The Thevenin equivalent seen by the galvanometer is determined first. <strong>(i) Calculation of Thevenin voltage</strong><br/><img src="img/e8c7beb9ee321f56.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,192px)"> Node voltage \(V_a\) is, \[V_a
=\frac{4\times1010}{1000+1010}
=\frac{404}{201}\ \text{V}\] Node voltage \(V_b\) is, \[V_b
=\frac{4\times1000}{1000+1000}
=2\ \text{V}\] Therefore, \[V_{th}
=V_a-V_b
=\frac{404}{201}-2
=\frac{2}{201}\ \text{V}
\approx 0.01\ \text{V}\] <strong>(ii) Calculation of Thevenin resistance</strong><br/>For finding \(R_{th}\), the independent voltage source is short-circuited. <img src="img/8a7497bd4077bb4b.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,288px)"> \[R_{th}
=(1000\parallel1010)+(1000\parallel1000)\] \[R_{th}
=\frac{1000\times1010}{1000+1010}+500
=502.49+500
=1002.49~\Omega\] The Thevenin equivalent circuit across the galvanometer is <img src="img/b65ccd30654ab96f.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,352px)"> \[I_G
=\frac{V_{th}}{R_{th}+R_G}
=\frac{\frac{2}{201}}{1002.49+100}\] \[I_G
=9.07\times10^{-6}\ \text{A}
=9.07\ \mu\text{A}\] \[\boxed{I_G \approx 9~\mu\text{A}}\]

Given: \(x(t)=e^{j9t}+e^{j5t}\) where \(j=\sqrt{-1}\), \[|x(t)|=\left|e^{j9t}+e^{j5t}\right|\] \[e^{j\theta}=\cos\theta+j\sin\theta\] \[x(t)=\cos9t+j\sin9t+\cos5t+j\sin5t\] \[|x(t)|=\sqrt{(\cos5t+\cos9t)^2+(\sin5t+\sin9t)^2}\] \[|x(t)|=
\sqrt{
\cos^25t+\cos^29t+2\cos5t\cos9t
+\sin^25t+\sin^29t+2\sin5t\sin9t
}\] \[|x(t)|=
\sqrt{
(1+1+2\cos5t\cos9t+2\sin5t\sin9t)
}\] \[|x(t)|=\sqrt{2+2\cos4t}\] \[|x(t)|=\sqrt{2(1+\cos4t)}\] \[|x(t)|=\sqrt{2(1+2\cos^22t-1)}\] \[|x(t)|=\sqrt{2\times2\cos^22t}\] \[|x(t)|=|2\cos2t|\] The fundamental period of \(\cos(2t)\) is, \[T=\frac{2\pi}{2}=\pi\] But for \(|\cos(2t)|\), the period is halved. \[T=\frac{\pi}{2}\ \text{sec}\] (B) \(\dfrac{\pi}{2}\)

For an ideal op-amp operating with negative feedback, \[V_{+}=V_{-}\] The non-inverting input voltage is obtained using the voltage divider formed by \(R_{3}\) and \(R_{4}\): \[V_{+}=\frac{R_{4}}{R_{3}+R_{4}}\,V\] <img src="img/f94daaa6334b9856.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,256px)"> Hence, \[V_{-}=\frac{R_{4}}{R_{3}+R_{4}}\,V\] Applying KCL at the inverting node, \[\frac{V-V_{-}}{R_{1}}=\frac{V_{-}-V_{out}}{R_{2}}\] The source current is \[I=\frac{V-V_{-}}{R_{1}}+\frac{V-V_{+}}{R_{3}}\] Since \(V_{+}=V_{-}=\dfrac{R_{4}}{R_{3}+R_{4}}V\), \[I=\frac{V}{R_{3}+R_{4}}+\frac{R_{3}}{R_{1}(R_{3}+R_{4})}V\] \[I=\frac{V(R_{1}+R_{3})}{R_{1}(R_{3}+R_{4})}\] Therefore, \[\frac{V}{I}
=\frac{R_{1}(R_{3}+R_{4})}{R_{1}+R_{3}}\] \[\boxed{\text{(a) }\frac{(R_{3}+R_{4})}{R_{1}+R_{3}}R
_{1}}\]

