The Zener diode fixes the base voltage at \[V_{B}=V_{Z}=4.7\,\text{V}\] Hence, \[V_{E}=V_{B}-V_{BE}=4.7-0.7=4.0\,\text{V}\] The emitter current is \[I_{E}=\frac{V_{E}}{R_{E}}
=\frac{4.0}{1\,\text{k}\Omega}
=4\,\text{mA}\] Since the transistor has very high \(\beta\), \[I_{B}\approx 0
\quad\Rightarrow\quad
I_{C}\approx I_{E}=4\,\text{mA}\] The LED is in series with the collector, therefore \[I_{\text{LED}}=I_{C}=4\,\text{mA}\] \[\boxed{4.00}\]

To find the zeros of the transfer function \(G(s)\), we determine the roots of its numerator polynomial: \[G(s) = 1 + \frac{2s - 1}{s^3 + 5s^2 + 3s + 22}  = \frac{s^3 + 5s^2 + 5s + 21}{s^3 + 5s^2 + 3s + 22}\] The numerator polynomial representing the zeros is: \[N(s) = s^3 + 5s^2 + 5s + 21 = 0\] <div class="sol-block sol-method"><div class="sol-block-title">Method 1</div><strong>Routh-Hurwitz Tabulation:</strong><br/>\[\begin{array}{c|cc}
s^3 &amp; 1 &amp; 5 \\
s^2 &amp; 5 &amp; 21 \\
s^1 &amp; \dfrac{5 \times 5 - 1 \times 21}{5} = \dfrac{4}{5} &amp; 0 \\
s^0 &amp; 21 &amp; 
\end{array}\] <ul><li>All elements in the first column are strictly positive, meaning there are <strong>no sign changes</strong>.</li><li>Thus, there are no roots in the right-half of the \(s\)-plane or the \(j\omega\)-axis.</li></ul> <div class="sol-block sol-method"><div class="sol-block-title">Method 2</div><strong>Shortcut Rule:</strong> For a standard \(3^{\text{rd}}\)-order characteristic equation: \(a s^3 + b s^2 + c s + d = 0\) The condition for stability is: \[\text{Inner Product} &gt; \text{Outer Product}\] \[b \times c &gt; a \times d\] \[a = 1, \quad b = 5, \quad c = 5, \quad d = 21\] \[5 \times 5 &gt; 1 \times 21\] Thus, there are no roots in the right-half of the \(s\)-plane or the \(j\omega\)-axis.</div></div> Since \(N(s)\) is a \(3^{\text{rd}}\)-order polynomial, all \(3\) roots must lie in the left-half of the \(s\)-plane. \[\boxed{(D) \ \ 3}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div>The number of sign changes in the first column of the Routh array equals the number of roots lying in the right-half of the \(s\)-plane (unstable roots).</div> <div class="sol-block sol-mistakes"><div class="sol-block-title">Mistakes to avoid</div><strong>Wrong Polynomial Selection:</strong> Do not accidentally perform Routh analysis on the characteristic equation (denominator).</div>
